# Not published, and not only mine to publish.
#
# _private/ holds the things that must not reach a public GitHub Pages site:
# unpublished reviews, including a student's thesis results, which are his to
# publish first. The app degrades deliberately when they are absent so that a
# checkout without them still runs.
#
# data/force_model.json is NO LONGER here. The trained surrogate is published
# deliberately: without it the deployed site can show no muscle force and no
# joint contact force at all, and there is no way to have that feature on a
# static site without shipping a model that reproduces it -- the head is 7% of
# the file and the trained trunk is the other 92%, so a "reduced" export
# publishes the model either way. That was a decision, not an oversight.
#
# data/reference.json stays out for a different reason from the rest: one of
# its three cases is a recording of a research participant (pullup_P01), and
# consent to record is not consent to publish. The two synthetic cases carry
# no such question.
#
# Anything the browser can fetch from the deployed site is public, whatever
# this file says: that is a property of static hosting, not of the repo.
_private/
data/review/
data/reference.json
Claude outputs
# The vision-model benchmark's inputs and outputs (tests/vision_model/README.md).
#
# Video of an athlete and the marker data that goes with it are a consented
# recording of a person, and consent to record is not consent to publish -- the
# same reason data/reference.json stays out. The downloaded .task files are
# 5-30 MB of someone else's weights, and results/ is regenerated by a run.
# The manifest that ties them together is per-machine; manifest.example.json is
# the one that is committed.
tests/vision_model/clips/*
!tests/vision_model/clips/manifest.example.json
tests/vision_model/gt/*
!tests/vision_model/gt/.gitkeep
tests/vision_model/models/
tests/vision_model/results/
